# Provenance — charts/README.md § `SOURCE` is required, including when nothing was forked.
#
# ADR-010: "Their charts are a starting point, not a dependency. Where a chart is close, fork it into
# charts/ with the upstream commit recorded in a SOURCE file." Nothing was forked here, and
# `vendored: none` is an answer to "where did this come from" where a missing file is not.

vendored: none
upstream: https://github.com/rabbitmq/cluster-operator

# The CRD contract these templates and CyberCloud.Providers.Messaging.Contracts render against.
# ⚠ Must agree with RabbitmqClusters.ClusterKind, including the plural — `rabbitmqclusters` is what
# addresses a REST path and it is not derivable from the kind. ⚠ The KIND IS `RabbitmqCluster`:
# capital R, lower-case `mq`, which is not how the product is spelled anywhere else including this
# type's own display name. A kind the API server does not know is a 404 at apply time, per object.
upstream-api: rabbitmq.com/v1beta1
upstream-version: 2.22.4
upstream-checked: 2026-08-12

# ⚠ THERE IS EXACTLY ONE SERVED VERSION AND IT IS THE STORED ONE. `v1beta1`, `served: true`,
# `storage: true`, no `deprecated` flag. So unlike a CRD mid-migration there is no second version a
# read-back could arrive in, and `Matches` does not have to tolerate two shapes.

# ⚠ THE CRD DECLARES `default:` VALUES, WHICH IS THE OPPOSITE OF WHAT charts/managed/kafka/SOURCE
# RECORDS ABOUT STRIMZI, AND IT IS THE FACT `RabbitmqClusters.Matches` IS BUILT ON. Read out of
# config/crd/bases/rabbitmq.com_rabbitmqclusters.yaml rather than out of the operator's README, which
# does not mention them. At depth ≤ 4, outside the embedded corev1 subtrees:
#
#   spec.replicas                        1
#   spec.persistence                     {"storage": "10Gi"}        ← an OBJECT-level default
#   spec.persistence.storage             "10Gi"
#   spec.service                         {"type": "ClusterIP"}      ← an OBJECT-level default
#   spec.service.type                    "ClusterIP"
#   spec.resources                       {"limits":{"cpu":"2000m","memory":"2Gi"},
#                                         "requests":{"cpu":"1000m","memory":"2Gi"}}
#   spec.terminationGracePeriodSeconds   604800
#   spec.delayStartSeconds               30
#   spec.imagePullSecrets[].name         ""
#   spec.secretBackend.externalSecret.name  ""
#
# ⚠ THE TWO OBJECT-LEVEL ONES ARE THE DANGEROUS SHAPE: omitting `spec.persistence` entirely still
# materialises `{storage: 10Gi}`, so "we did not ask for a volume" and "we asked for 10Gi" are the
# same request.
#
# ⚠ AND `spec.resources` IS BURSTABLE. requests (1000m) sit below limits (2000m), which is
# Kubernetes' Burstable QoS class, while docs/plan/12 § Sizing vocabulary defines the c1 family as
# GUARANTEED. So a rendered object that omitted `resources` would not get "no resources" — it would
# get a different QoS class from the one the preset name promises, silently, from the API server.
# RabbitmqClusters.ClusterJson writes the block for that reason.
#
# ⚠ THERE IS NO DEFAULT ON `spec.image`, AND IT STILL ARRIVES. See the webhook note below.

# ⚠ THE OPERATOR EDITS `spec`, THROUGH A MUTATING ADMISSION WEBHOOK — which no other operator in
# charts/managed/ does, and which is a second and independent reason `Matches` must be containment.
#
#   * MutatingWebhookConfiguration at /mutate-rabbitmq-com-v1beta1-rabbitmqcluster, CREATE+UPDATE,
#     failurePolicy: Fail. Registered in cmd/main.go unless ENABLE_WEBHOOKS=false, so it is ON in the
#     released bundle. It writes spec.image, spec.imagePullSecrets and — under Vault —
#     spec.secretBackend.vault.defaultUserUpdaterImage when each is unset.
#   * ValidatingWebhookConfiguration at /validate-rabbitmq-com-v1beta1-rabbitmqcluster, same policy.
#     It refuses hostPID / hostNetwork / hostIPC / serviceAccountName / hostPath volumes and a list of
#     container security-context escalations under spec.override.statefulSet.
#   * ⚠ BOTH NEED CERT-MANAGER. config/default/kustomization.yaml includes ../webhook and
#     ../certmanager. A cluster with the CRD installed and no webhook certificate refuses EVERY
#     create and update — which is a different failure from a missing CRD and needs to be readable as
#     one. Since 2026-08-12 an unmapped 4xx comes back naming the API server's own message.
#   * The controller also writes back onto metadata: the annotations rabbitmq.com/version,
#     rabbitmq.com/erlang-version and rabbitmq.com/queueRebalanceNeededAt, plus the finalizer
#     deletion.finalizers.rabbitmqclusters.rabbitmq.com.
#
# ⚠ WHAT IT DOES *NOT* DO IS PREPEND TO THE TENANT'S CONFIG, and that was checked because it is
# exactly the hazard ValkeyCaches found in spotahome's validator. The operator writes its own
# defaults to /etc/rabbitmq/conf.d/10-operatorDefaults.conf and the value of
# spec.rabbitmq.additionalConfig to 90-userDefinedConfiguration.conf — two files RabbitMQ merges by
# filename order. So the string round-trips through spec verbatim, and 90 winning over 10 is what
# makes `default_queue_type = quorum` take effect over the operator's own block.
#
# The operator's own 10-operatorDefaults.conf, for the record, is:
#
#   log.console = true
#   queue_master_locator = min-masters
#   disk_free_limit.absolute = 2GB
#   cluster_partition_handling = pause_minority
#   cluster_formation.peer_discovery_backend = rabbit_peer_discovery_k8s
#   cluster_formation.k8s.host = kubernetes.default
#   cluster_formation.k8s.address_type = hostname
#   cluster_formation.target_cluster_size_hint = <spec.replicas>
#   cluster_name = <metadata.name>
#   auth_mechanisms.1 = PLAIN
#   auth_mechanisms.2 = AMQPLAIN          ← ANONYMOUS is deliberately absent

# ⚠ THE `guest` USER, WHICH IS THE FIRST THING ANYONE ASKS ABOUT RABBITMQ AND IS NOT A PROBLEM HERE.
# RabbitMQ ships with guest/guest. A cluster this operator creates never has a `guest` user at all:
# internal/resource/default_user_secret.go generates a username of `default_user_` + 24 random bytes
# and a 24-byte random password, writes them into the Secret `<name>-default-user`, and mounts them
# as /etc/rabbitmq/conf.d/11-default_user.conf — which the broker reads on FIRST BOOT to seed its one
# user. Because that user is not named `guest`, RabbitMQ's own `loopback_users.guest = true` never
# applies to it and `guest` is never created to be restricted. The operator does not touch
# `loopback_users` at all; grepping the repository for it returns nothing.
#
# ⚠ SO THIS ROW'S `listKeys` GAP IS KAFKA-SHAPED RATHER THAN NATS-SHAPED, and the distinction is what
# charts/managed/nats/conformance.yaml § owed asks to be made. That file records that nats-server
# with no authorization block accepts every connection in the namespace — a service that comes up
# OPEN. This one comes up AUTHENTICATED with a credential the platform did not choose and cannot yet
# read. See conformance.yaml § owed, `listkeys-has-no-handler`.
#
# The Secret's keys are: username, password, default_user.conf, provider, type, host, port and
# connection_string, plus one port key per protocol plugin enabled. It implements the Service Binding
# "Provisioned Service" spec, which is why `connection_string` is there without anyone asking.

# ⚠ THE CONFIGURATION SURFACE THAT MATTERS TO THIS ROW IS A STRING, NOT A FIELD.
# `default_queue_type` — the setting docs/plan/12's RabbitMQ row is entirely about — has no member on
# this CRD. The only route to it is spec.rabbitmq.additionalConfig, a free-text INI string constrained
# by `maxLength: 100000` and nothing else. Sibling fields, with their limits:
#
#   spec.rabbitmq.additionalConfig    string, maxLength 100000
#   spec.rabbitmq.additionalPlugins   array of string, MaxItems 100
#   spec.rabbitmq.advancedConfig      string, maxLength 100000
#   spec.rabbitmq.envConfig           string, maxLength 100000, and the CRD's ONLY CEL rule:
#                                     !self.contains('$(') && !self.contains('`')
#   spec.rabbitmq.erlangInetConfig    string, maxLength 2000
#
# `default_queue_type` is node-wide in rabbitmq.conf since 3.13.3 and a per-vhost setting overrides
# it (per-vhost DQT since 3.11). This row manages no vhosts, so what it guarantees is the default
# vhost's queues rather than every queue in the cluster — conformance.yaml § owed, `child-types`.

# ⚠ PLUGINS THE OPERATOR ALWAYS ENABLES AND THE CRD CANNOT DISABLE — internal/resource/rabbitmq_plugins.go
# `requiredPlugins`: rabbitmq_peer_discovery_k8s, rabbitmq_prometheus, rabbitmq_management.
# `spec.rabbitmq.additionalPlugins` is APPENDED to them. This is why values.yaml declares no
# `monitoring` block: rabbitmq_prometheus cannot be turned off, so a property whose false branch the
# platform could not honour would be a lie in the API rather than a setting.

# ⚠ THE CLIENT SERVICE CARRIES THREE PORTS AND `spec.service.type` IS ONE ENUM OVER ALL OF THEM.
# internal/resource/service.go: amqp 5672, management 15672 and prometheus 15692 are unconditional,
# plus one port per recognised protocol plugin. `spec.service` has exactly four members —
# annotations, ipFamilyPolicy, labels, type — and `loadBalancerSourceRanges` is NOT among them; it
# lives only under spec.override.service.spec, which the operator applies as a STRATEGIC MERGE PATCH
# against corev1.ServiceSpec. That single fact is why this row declares no external listener: see
# conformance.yaml § owed, `external-exposure-moves-three-ports`.
#
# Service names, read off the operator rather than guessed: ServiceSuffix is the empty string and
# ChildResourceName trims the trailing hyphen, so the client Service is named exactly like the custom
# resource, and the headless one is `<name>-nodes` carrying 4369 (epmd) and 25672 (cluster rpc).

# ⚠ FOUR THINGS THE OPERATOR REFUSES OR PERFORMS DESTRUCTIVELY, none of which is visible from the CRD
# schema and all of which change what a reconciler may promise:
#
#   * A SCALE-DOWN IS ACCEPTED AT THE API SERVER AND THEN REFUSED. internal/controller/reconcile_scale_down.go
#     logs, emits an UnsupportedOperation Warning event and sets status.conditions[ReconcileSuccess]
#     to False — the write itself SUCCEEDS. So spec and the live StatefulSet diverge permanently and
#     a read-back of spec.replicas reports the new value. conformance.yaml § owed carries this.
#   * Scaling to zero is supported and remembers the previous count; scaling back up to any other
#     number is refused.
#   * `spec.persistence.storage` cannot be SHRUNK — internal/scaling/scaling.go, "shrinking persistent
#     volumes is not supported" — and GROWING it deletes the StatefulSet (orphan delete) before
#     resizing the PVCs.
#   * `spec.persistence.storage` is x-kubernetes-int-or-string, so `20` and `"20Gi"` are both valid
#     and mean different things. RabbitmqClusters.ClusterJson writes a string for that reason.

# ⚠ NEITHER A PodMonitor NOR A ServiceMonitor IS EMITTED BY THE OPERATOR — grepping internal/, api/
# and cmd/ finds only a comment. This is docs/plan/12 § The pattern, once, piece 6's SECOND branch
# again, and the third service to reach it: CloudNativePG answered the first branch with
# spec.monitoring.enablePodMonitor, Strimzi reached the second and left the object owed, and NATS
# reached it and DISCHARGED it because it owns its own pod labels. This row is back in Strimzi's
# position — the labels a selector would match are the operator's — so the object is owed rather than
# hand-written over somebody else's labels. Upstream ships examples under observability/prometheus/monitors/.
